    <node id="Internet">
      <data key="d0">Internet</data>
      <data key="d1">Technology</data>
      <data key="d2">The **Internet** is a multifaceted entity, encompassing a collection of concepts, techniques, media, agreements, protocols, and websites. It is fundamentally a global network, connecting billions of devices, including computers, and facilitating communication and data exchange. This network is based on the packet switching principle, inspired by Paul Baran's work. The Internet utilizes the Internet Protocol Suite (TCP/IP) to serve billions of users worldwide.

The Internet has undergone significant development and is a significant technological advancement, particularly in the 1990s, when organizations had to decide whether to adopt it. It provides various services and is used for sharing information, facilitating transactions, and bringing together supply and demand. It is also used to facilitate worldwide trade.

The Internet's evolution is sometimes compared to the emergence of blockchain technology. The text also mentions the promise of the internet to become more productive. However, it also notes that the Internet has not necessarily made us more productive or democratic.

In a historical context, the Internet is discussed as part of the Weconomics analysis. It is also used as an analogy to highlight the potential pitfalls of standardization, particularly the influence of large tech companies. Initially a distributed peer-to-peer platform, it has become largely centralized. The Internet is a specific technology mentioned as part of the 'New Digital Economy'.
</data>
